# Overview
#
# Grammar calls each phrase a concessive opener, because it grants the
# point before it and then turns away from it (`That said, the default
# suits most hosts`). The opener spends several words on a turn that
# `but` makes in one. The fix writes `but` or opens the sentence with
# the new point. The rule sits at Required, because it matches exact
# phrases and the fix is mechanical.
#
# Expression
#
# `that being said` and `this being said` count as written. `that said`,
# `having said that`, and `with that said` need the glued comma of an
# opener. `having said this` and `with this said` need the comma too,
# the way their `that` forms do.
#
# False Positives
#
# Without the comma, `that said` often opens a description of the noun
# before it (`the error that said the disk was full`), so it passes.
#
# Related Rules
#
# The Optional ProsePassive leaves `said` out after `being`, so only
# this rule reports `that being said`.
#
# Measurement
#
# The rule hit 2 lines in the measure.py corpus, and 2 of 2 sampled hits
# turned away from the point before them.
expr main [(that being said), (that said,), (having said that,), (with that said,), (this being said), (having said this,), (with this said,)]
let message "Concessive opener. Write `but`, or delete the phrase and state the point."
let description "Flags `that said` and similar openers that turn away from the point before them."
let becomes ""
let kind "Style"
let scope "Sentence"
allows "The error that said the disk was full came from the writer."
